---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/pharmacy/foundational/pharmaceutical-sciences-foundations">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Pharmacy is the science of medicines from molecule to patient, and its foundational
    insight is that a drug is not a substance so much as a <em>journey</em>: a molecule that
    must survive manufacture, dissolve, cross membranes, dodge enzymes, find its receptor,
    do its work, and leave — in the right amounts, at the right times, in the right tissues.
    Two paired disciplines describe the trip. <em>Pharmacokinetics</em> is what the body
    does to the drug, summarised in four letters: ADME — absorption, distribution,
    metabolism, excretion. <em>Pharmacodynamics</em> is what the drug does to the body:
    which receptors it touches and how strongly. Between those two sits every dosing
    decision, every interaction warning, and every "take with food" instruction a
    pharmacist gives.
  </p>
  <p>
    Behind the journey stands the molecule itself. Medicinal chemistry explains why tiny
    structural changes transform a drug's behaviour — the same skeleton that calms a
    wheeze in one arrangement can race a heart in another — and the study of those
    relationships (structure–activity relationships, SAR) is how modern drugs are designed
    rather than discovered by luck. The pharmacist's daily craft assumes all three layers:
    chemistry (what the molecule is), pharmacokinetics (where it goes), pharmacodynamics
    (what it does on arrival).
  </p>
  <p>
    The stakes of these sciences are easy to state. Medicines are the most common
    intervention in healthcare — most older adults take several daily — and they are also
    a leading cause of avoidable harm, almost never through mysterious chemistry and
    almost always through the predictable machinery this chapter describes: a dose set for
    kidneys a patient no longer has, an interaction nobody checked, a formulation crushed
    against its design, a half-life ignored in a taper. Pharmacy's claim to be a distinct
    clinical profession rests on owning this machinery — seeing in a prescription list not
    just names and doses but journeys, clocks and receptors, each with a failure mode that
    is knowable in advance.
  </p>
  <p>
    This chapter walks the layers in order: the molecule and its SAR, the body's four ADME
    legs with the half-life arithmetic that runs every prescription, the receptor theory
    that turns concentrations into effects, and the formulation science that packages the
    whole journey into a tablet, a patch or an infusion bag.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="ADME: the drug's journey in four legs" widget="equationStep" config={admeCfg} />

  <h2>Medicinal chemistry and SAR</h2>
  <p>
    Receptors are proteins with binding pockets, and molecules bind by shape, charge and
    flexibility — the lock-and-key intuition, refined into quantitative science. Change the
    key slightly and you change everything: potency, selectivity, duration, even whether
    the drug turns the receptor on or blocks it. The systematic study of these changes —
    structure–activity relationships — is the engine of drug design: make one structural
    modification at a time, measure the effect, and learn which parts of the molecule do
    which job.
  </p>
  <p>
    A classic worked example is the asthma inhaler. Adrenaline relieves bronchospasm
    powerfully — but it stimulates all adrenergic receptors, so the relief arrives with
    racing heart and tremor. Medicinal chemistry untangled the receptor family (β1 mostly
    cardiac, β2 mostly bronchial) and then modified the structure to favour β2: a change
    to the ring (saligenin instead of catechol) plus a bulkier N-substituent produced
    salbutamol, which is β2-selective enough to open airways at doses that largely spare
    the heart. Same target organ, same disease, dramatically better drug — achieved purely
    by reasoning from structure to selectivity. SAR also polices what <em>cannot</em> be
    changed: the pharmacophore, the minimal arrangement of features the receptor actually
    reads. Everything else is negotiable, and most optimisation is rearranging the
    negotiable parts to fix absorption, metabolism or patent life.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rules of thumb came later to guide the search for <em>druggable</em> molecules.
    Lipinski's "rule of five" flags oral absorption trouble when a molecule exceeds roughly
    500 in molecular weight, 5 hydrogen-bond donors, 10 hydrogen-bond acceptors, or a logP
    of 5 — a membrane must be crossed, and membranes are choosy. The numbers are heuristics,
    routinely broken by successful exceptions, but they capture the central tension of
    medicinal chemistry: a drug must be water-soluble enough to travel in blood and
    fat-soluble enough to cross lipid membranes — two demands that pull in opposite
    directions on every molecule ever made.
  </p>

  <h2>Pharmacokinetics: ADME</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Absorption</em> is entry into the blood. An intravenous dose is 100 percent
    bioavailable by definition; an oral dose must survive stomach acid, cross the gut wall,
    and — the great filter — survive the liver's first-pass metabolism, which can consume
    most of the dose before it reaches circulation. <em>Distribution</em> is delivery to
    tissues: blood flow leads, but protein binding (only unbound drug is active and
    clearable), fat solubility (the brain's fatty membranes admit lipophilic drugs; the
    same property makes them linger in adipose tissue) and molecular size set the map.
    <em>Metabolism</em> is chemical alteration, mostly by the liver's cytochrome P450 (CYP)
    enzyme family — usually deactivating the drug, sometimes activating it (prodrugs like
    codeine must be converted to work), occasionally creating the toxic surprise.
    <em>Excretion</em> removes drug and metabolites, overwhelmingly through the kidneys —
    which is why renal impairment is the classic reason for dose reduction: the pipe
    narrows, so the flow must.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bioavailability earns a worked example, because it rewrites what a "dose" means.
    Morphine given intravenously is fully bioavailable; the same 10 mg swallowed survives
    first-pass metabolism poorly — oral bioavailability is roughly 20 to 40 percent — so
    the oral dose must be two to five times larger to reach the same blood levels.
    Propranolol, the classic first-pass casualty, loses so much to hepatic metabolism that
    its oral dose is several times its intravenous one. None of this is inefficiency to be
    fixed; it is the body's border control doing its job — and the entire industry of
    "equivalent dose" conversions between routes is the arithmetic of that border, made
    tabulated and safe.
  </p>

  <h2>Half-life is the prescription's clock</h2>
  <p>
    Elimination is exponential: each half-life removes half of whatever remains. A drug
    with a 4-hour half-life is at 50 percent after 4 hours, 25 percent after 8, about 6
    percent after 16 — the plotted curve. Read forward, the same arithmetic sets every
    dosing schedule: dose at intervals near the half-life and the peaks and troughs
    plateau — <em>steady state</em> — after roughly five half-lives (about 20 hours here),
    regardless of the dose size. This is why "take twice daily" is arithmetic rather than
    ritual, why a drug reaches full effect on a timescale no instruction can shorten, and
    why stopping a long-half-life drug (say, fluoxetine, whose half-life is measured in
    days) fades slowly and gracefully while stopping a short one (a benzodiazepine measured
    in hours) does not.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="exponential elimination with a 4-hour half-life" widget="plotter" config={halfLifeCfg} />

  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> half-life equals duration of effect. It equals the
    fall of drug <em>concentration</em>, and effects can outlast it dramatically. Aspirin's
    plasma half-life is about 20 minutes, yet a single dose disables platelets for their
    entire 7-to-10-day lifespan — the molecule binds irreversibly, so the effect persists
    after the drug is long gone. Duration of action is a pharmacodynamic property, set by
    receptor residency and physiology, not by concentration alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Interactions are mostly CYP politics</h2>
  <p>
    The CYP enzyme family metabolises a large share of all drugs, and enzymes are shared
    resources — which makes them the main arena of drug–drug interactions. A second drug
    can <em>inhibit</em> an enzyme (traffic light to red): inhibit CYP3A4 and a co-
    administered statin accumulates toward muscle damage. Or <em>induce</em> it (green
    light): the enzyme churns faster and clears the other drug before it works — the
    documented way St John's wort undermines oral contraceptives. Food plays too: grapefruit
    juice inhibits CYP3A4 in the gut wall for a day or more, amplifying anything that
    passes through. The pharmacist's interaction check is applied enzymology — the moment
    ADME theory becomes a life-saving reflex.
  </p>

  <h2>Pharmacodynamics and receptors</h2>
  <p>
    Pharmacodynamics converts concentration into effect, and its central object is the
    dose–response curve: as concentration rises, effect climbs in a familiar sigmoid when
    plotted against log-dose — few receptors occupied at low dose, near-saturation at high.
    Two numbers characterise a drug on that curve. <em>Potency</em> (the EC50, the
    concentration giving half-maximal effect) says how much drug it takes;
    <em>efficacy</em> (the ceiling, Emax) says how much effect is possible even in
    principle. The distinction matters clinically: a potent drug needs a small dose, but
    only a high-efficacy drug can produce a large effect — and no dose of a partial
    agonist will ever match a full agonist at the same receptor.
  </p>
  <p>
    Receptor theory also classifies the cast. <em>Agonists</em> activate (morphine at the
    μ-opioid receptor); <em>antagonists</em> occupy without activating, blocking the
    endogenous signal (naloxone evicting opioids to reverse an overdose — competitive,
    so it must itself be re-dosed as the opioid outlasts it); <em>partial agonists</em> do
    both at once, topping down an overactive system while substituting for a missing one
    (buprenorphine's pharmacological trick). Add the <em>therapeutic index</em> — the ratio
    of toxic to effective dose — and pharmacodynamics delivers the risk framework: drugs
    with narrow indices (warfarin, digoxin, lithium, insulin) demand monitoring of levels,
    while wide-index drugs forgive more.
  </p>

  <h2>Formulation: the science of the dosage form</h2>
  <p>
    The same molecule performs differently as tablet, capsule, suspension, patch or
    infusion — formulation is the engineering of absorption. Dissolution rate gates how
    fast a solid can begin; coatings decide where in the gut it releases; excipients —
    the "inactive" ingredients — control flow, stability and disintegration; modified-
    release designs stretch one dose over 24 hours by engineering the exit ramp. Sterile
    preparations add asepsis as an absolute requirement, since injections bypass every
    defence the body owns. Bioavailability — the fraction of dose reaching circulation,
    and the speed of its arrival — is where chemistry, manufacturing and patient technique
    meet; it is why the pharmacist asks not just "what drug?" but "what form, what
    brand substitution, with or without food?" and why a crushed extended-release tablet
    can be an overdose delivered slowly on purpose, all at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Pharmacokinetics earns its mathematics in the concepts of clearance and volume of
    distribution. Clearance (flow of blood fully cleared of drug per unit time) is the
    constant that connects dose rate to steady-state concentration; volume of distribution
    (apparent, not anatomical — a drug that hides in fat can "occupy" hundreds of litres)
    connects concentration to total amount in the body; half-life is their ratio. From
    these three, therapeutic drug monitoring derives its dose adjustments in renal
    failure, obesity, the critically ill and the very young — populations in whom the
    "standard adult" assumptions quietly fail.
  </p>
  <p>
    The frontiers extend each layer. Pharmacogenomics explains the outliers: CYP2D6 poor
    metabolisers get no analgesia from codeine (it never converts to morphine) and
    ultrarapid metabolisers get too much — the same 30 mg is three different therapies,
    which is why genotype is creeping onto labels. Prodrug design exploits metabolism on
    purpose, smuggling an active molecule past absorption barriers as an inactive one.
    Antibiotic dosing divides by pattern — time-above-MIC for beta-lactams versus
    concentration-dependent killing for aminoglycosides — so the same goal (kill bacteria,
    spare the patient) runs on opposite schedules. And the open problems are pressing:
    predicting metabolism from structure before humans are exposed, designing around
    resistance, and dosing the new biologics — large proteins whose kinetics obey rules
    the small-molecule century never wrote.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NAPLEX Competency Statements (NABP); Katzung, <em>Basic &amp; Clinical Pharmacology</em>; Aulton's <em>Pharmaceutics</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
